(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ophthalmology-i-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Common Ophthalmological Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 68-year-old man complains that his upper lids feel heavy and he is looking through his lashes. There are excess folds of skin over both upper lids. Which assessment most directly determines whether surgery will be covered?","choices":["Visual field testing","Intraocular pressure measurement","Slit lamp examination with fluorescein","Computed tomography of the orbits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a demonstrated field deficit is what the deck ties to insurance coverage for blepharoplasty. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A healthy 28-year-old man has three days of a red right eye with thick yellow discharge and lids stuck together in the morning. Vision is normal, the cornea is clear, and there is no preauricular node. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical histamine blocker with a mast cell stabiliser","Oral aciclovir for ten days","A topical broad-spectrum antibiotic such as a fluoroquinolone","Oral doxycycline twice daily for seven days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — an immunocompetent adult with uncomplicated bacterial conjunctivitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has a swollen, red, tender left eyelid and fever after sinusitis. The globe is white, the pupil reacts normally, and eye movements are full and painless with normal vision. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Pre-septal (periorbital) cellulitis","Post-septal (orbital) cellulitis","Dacryocystitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a white globe with full painless movements and normal vision places the infection in front of the septum. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has had viral conjunctivitis for four weeks and now reports significant photophobia and blurred vision. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer to ophthalmology","Reassure and continue cool compresses","Start a topical antibiotic","Start a topical corticosteroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — beyond three weeks, or with photophobia or decreased vision after onset, the deck refers. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman reports a constant foreign body sensation in one eye. The lower lid margin turns inward and the lashes contact the globe. Which examination is most appropriate?","choices":["Slit lamp examination to assess whether the cornea has been involved","Computed tomography of the orbits and sinuses with contrast","Fluorescein angiography","Serum lipid profile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the question with entropion is always whether the lashes have damaged the cornea. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has had a red left eye with stringy mucoid discharge for six weeks. Topical antibiotics have not helped, and the lower palpebral conjunctiva shows follicles (pale bumps that are redder at the base). Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Slit lamp examination with fluorescein staining","Computed tomography of the orbits with contrast","Conjunctival scraping for potassium hydroxide preparation","Conjunctival nucleic acid amplification testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the chronic course, the follicles and the failure of topical therapy point to chlamydial disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man is considering laser ablation for xanthelasma. What should he be told about the outcome?","choices":["The lesions will resolve on their own if left alone","The lesions will become malignant if not removed","Even after effective local treatment, recurrences are common","Local treatment is curative and recurrence is rare"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck flags recurrence explicitly. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a firm, painless lump in her upper eyelid that has been slowly enlarging for three weeks. It is not tender. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Incision and drainage today","Oral antibiotics for ten days","Urgent referral to exclude sebaceous carcinoma","Warm compresses with gentle massage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the painless, slowly enlarging nodule is a chalazion, managed conservatively first. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a painful dermatomal rash across one side of his forehead has corneal lesions on fluorescein that branch but lack elevated edges and have no knobs at the branch tips. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial keratitis","Herpes zoster keratitis","Herpes simplex keratitis","Acanthamoeba keratitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pseudodendrites lack the elevated edges and terminal end bulbs of a true dendrite. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with a pterygium asks whether lubricating drops and sunglasses will get rid of it. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["Conservative management may control symptoms but will not make the lesion resolve","Drops and sun protection will clear the lesion within a few months","It will resolve on its own once sun exposure stops","Only oral medication will shrink it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck is explicit on this point. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 38-year-old contact lens wearer resists opening a painful watering eye. There is a ring of redness at the corneal edge (ciliary flush) and a white spot on the cornea with surrounding irregularity. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent referral within 24 hours","Routine referral within two weeks","Reassurance and lubricating drops","Emergent referral to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck escalates a corneal ulcer above keratitis's 24-hour urgency to emergent. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has pain, redness and swelling confined to the outer third of his upper eyelid, with an enlarged node in front of the ear on the same side. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pre-septal cellulitis","Hordeolum","Dacryoadenitis","Dacryocystitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lacrimal gland sits superotemporally, and preauricular nodes are described. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has completed a two-week lid hygiene regimen for blepharitis with no improvement. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start a topical corticosteroid","Try a topical antibiotic","Go straight to oral antibiotics","Refer urgently to ophthalmology today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck escalates from lid hygiene to topical antibiotics, then to oral if needed. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man is starting warm compresses for a chalazion. What should he be told about the timescale?","choices":["It will not improve without surgery","Improvement may take months","It should be gone within 48 hours","It should be gone within one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck says so, and setting this expectation prevents a premature return. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has a tender lid margin nodule, and the surrounding lid and periorbital skin are now diffusely swollen, red and warm. The globe is white with full painless movements. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Continue warm compresses alone for another two weeks","Refer for immediate incision and drainage of the nodule","Start topical antibiotic drops","Treat as pre-septal cellulitis with systemic antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck says to treat an associated pre-septal cellulitis per that pathway. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has begun treatment for a corneal ulcer. What should he be told about follow-up and healing?","choices":["He needs no follow-up, and it should heal in 48 hours","He needs review in one month, and it should heal within a year","He needs review in two weeks, and it will not heal without surgery","He needs review the next day, and most ulcers heal over two to three weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — next-day follow-up after starting an anti-infective, with a two to three week course. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman developed a painful, tender, red lump at the margin of her lower eyelid overnight. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hordeolum","Chalazion","Dacryocystitis","Xanthelasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — acute onset over 24 hours, tender, and at the lid margin. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 17-year-old has bilateral itchy red eyes every spring with swollen lids and stringy watery discharge. Vision is normal. Which single topical agent both blocks histamine and stabilises mast cells?","choices":["Artificial tears alone","Olopatadine","A topical fluoroquinolone","A topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck names it as doing both. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fisherman has a fleshy triangular growth on the nasal conjunctiva of one eye that now extends a short distance onto the cornea. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pterygium","Pinguecula","Corneal ulcer","Chalazion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — corneal extension is what makes it a pterygium rather than a pinguecula. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has severe deep boring eye pain that wakes her at night and radiates to her cheek. The sclera has a bluish-purple hue (violaceous discolouration, the choroid showing through thinned sclera). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Artificial tears and an oral non-steroidal anti-inflammatory, review in two days","Topical antibiotic drops and review in one week","Reassurance that it is self-limited","Urgent referral to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the sclera is at risk of perforation and may need a surgical patch. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman from a trachoma-endemic region has eyelashes turned inward and abrading her cornea after years of repeated infection. Which is required?","choices":["Lubricating drops alone","Observation with annual review","Surgical treatment of the trichiasis","A further single dose of azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck states that trichiasis requires surgery; antibiotics cannot reverse scarring. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman wakes with severe pain, photophobia and blurred vision in one eye after sleeping in her contact lenses. Fluorescein shows a central epithelial defect with a white infiltrate beneath it. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start a topical corticosteroid drop and review him again in one week","Remove the lenses without patching and arrange same-day ophthalmology evaluation","Patch the eye and review her in two days","Dispense a topical anaesthetic for comfort at home"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is microbial keratitis until proven otherwise, and it is a same-day condition. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had three separate episodes of anterior uveitis in two years and reports joint pain and lower back stiffness. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start long-term prophylactic topical antibiotics","Arrange fluorescein angiography","Thorough systemic evaluation for an underlying autoimmune condition","Reassure him that recurrence is expected and needs no further workup"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck calls for this when uveitis is recurrent or has suggestive systemic features. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman is anxious about the dye for fluorescein angiography because she reacted to contrast for a scan once. What is accurate to tell her?","choices":["Fluorescein contains no iodine and is relatively safe","It contains iodine, so her previous reaction is a contraindication","It is radioactive and requires shielding","It is injected into the eye itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck notes the absence of iodine explicitly. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a swollen red eyelid, fever, and pain on moving the eye. The globe is proptotic and she has double vision. Which investigation is most appropriate?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Computed tomography of the orbits and paranasal sinuses with contrast","Slit lamp examination with fluorescein alone","Conjunctival nucleic acid amplification testing for chlamydia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — post-septal features mean orbital involvement must be imaged. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis has severe deep eye pain that wakes her at night and radiates to her face. There is a bluish-purple patch on the white of the eye and the vessels will not move with a cotton-tip applicator. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Viral conjunctivitis","Scleritis","Episcleritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — night pain, violaceous hue and immobile vessels, on an autoimmune background. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 3-day-old infant has markedly swollen lids and copious purulent discharge from both eyes, with a palpable preauricular node. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Oral erythromycin for fourteen days as an outpatient","Warm compresses and reassurance that it will settle","Emergent referral and hospital admission for systemic ceftriaxone, cultures and Gram stain","Topical fluoroquinolone drops with a review appointment the following day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — suspected gonococcal conjunctivitis in a neonate risks corneal perforation and is an emergency. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with confirmed non-infectious anterior uveitis is under ophthalmology care. Which treatment does the lecture describe as typical?","choices":["Intraocular corticosteroid injection","Oral antivirals for ten days","Topical fluoroquinolone","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — for non-infectious anterior disease the deck gives topical steroids. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a penetrating eye injury from a metal fragment and a suspected open globe. Which action should be taken?","choices":["Irrigate copiously before doing anything else","Place a rigid eye shield, avoid any pressure or tonometry, keep him nil by mouth, and obtain emergency ophthalmology consultation","Measure the intraocular pressure first to assess the extent of damage","Patch the eye firmly and arrange next-day review"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the notes' protective sequence for a suspected open globe. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman presents 48 hours after a dermatomal vesicular rash appeared over her forehead and upper lid. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Start an oral antiviral now and refer for slit lamp examination and dilated fundoscopy","Wait for ophthalmology to confirm ocular involvement before starting any antiviral","Start a topical corticosteroid to reduce inflammation","Reassure her that no treatment is needed for a dermatomal rash"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — within the ideal 72-hour window, and the eye still needs examining. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"}]};
